<div
  data-widget="orthogonal-rotation"
  data-reveal
  class="not-prose border border-slate-800 rounded-lg p-5 my-8 bg-slate-900/30"
>
  <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-slate-500 mb-4">Interactive &middot; Orthogonal Basis</p>
  <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-6 items-center">
    <canvas class="border border-slate-800 rounded shrink-0"></canvas>
    <div class="flex-1 w-full">
      <label class="block text-sm text-slate-300 mb-2" for="theta-slider">Rotation angle θ</label>
      <input id="theta-slider" type="range" min="0" max="360" step="1" value="30" class="w-full accent-slate-300" />
      <p data-readout class="mt-3 text-sm font-mono text-slate-400"></p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
        v₁ and v₂ are the rows of the rotation matrix R<sub>z</sub>. Spin θ all the way around — the two arrows
        stay perpendicular and unit-length at every angle, which is exactly what makes R<sub>z</sub> orthogonal.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  import '../../../scripts/courses/widgets/orthogonal-rotation';
</script>
